00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:46.900
a big storage customer.

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:52.270
We are using some of the global
and multi-regional and regional

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:54.520
buckets primarily,
although we're

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:57.460
sort of moving towards some
of the Nearline and Coldline

00:09:57.460 --> 00:09:59.290
storage as well.

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:01.540
We do a massive
amount of compute--

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:02.590
parallel compute.

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:07.600
So we run jobs that at
times use, let's say,

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:11.330
tens of thousands of
CPUs simultaneously.

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:15.730
And so we're a big customer
of the preemptible VM service.

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:20.200
In fact, we were, I believe
Google's blog announcement

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:22.390
customer for that service.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:24.200
MARK MANDEL: And so
just as an example,

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:26.085
what would you be
using these things for?

00:10:26.085 --> 00:10:28.210
Like, you said you have a
wide variety of products.

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:29.920
But now I'm just quite curious,
like at a concrete level,

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:30.850
what you'd be doing.

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:32.530
LUKAS KARLSSON: So
the most common thing

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:36.320
that we do with computing
is we do data analysis.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:38.890
So we have all these
instruments that

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:41.050
are sequencing
DNA and generating

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:43.660
various kinds of
raw data from images

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:45.850
to other sorts of data files.

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:48.370
And we run these
pipelines against them,

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:52.150
which basically looks like
potentially a Python script

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:55.210
or something that
reads in some raw data,

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:58.510
runs some Unix tools
against that data,

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:01.060
and then takes the output
from that first step

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:03.790
and makes that the
input of the next step.

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:07.630
And then, flowing through
a pipeline of steps

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:10.420
to generate some certain
kind of output that helps

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:13.554
answer some particular genetic
question about the data.

00:11:13.554 --> 00:11:14.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:15.860
So I guess since we have a--

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:18.820
I was going to say a
broad idea of the product.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:20.580
Jokes.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:22.650
Maybe we can talk a
little bit about the fact

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:25.260
that you mentioned that you
are a platinum customer.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:26.230
What does this mean?

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:28.847
Maybe we can get Mike
to answer this question.

00:11:28.847 --> 00:11:29.680
MIKE ALTARACE: Sure.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:30.179
Yeah.

00:11:30.179 --> 00:11:32.530
So a platinum customer
is a strategic customer

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:33.970
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:37.540
We deem those customers to
be of high importance to us

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:38.540
as a cloud provider.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:42.040
And we want to make sure they
get the utmost experience

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:45.700
when it comes to using
Google Cloud Services.

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:48.040
And as part of that, to
make sure that that's really

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:49.930
happening is key.

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:53.850
A Strategic Customer Engineer
like myself is dedicatedly

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:55.900
assigned to those customers.

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:58.930
And in a way, we
are the constant

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:01.420
from the technical perspective
that they have in Google.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:04.690
I'm like their
technical ambassador,

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:09.040
bridging all the different
groups within Google Cloud,

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:12.670
the PMs, the engineering,
the operationals, the TAM,

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:15.730
the sales, support itself.

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:18.970
And through those, providing
technical advisory, product

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.230
influence, and assistance
with operations.

00:12:21.230 --> 00:12:23.710
So for example, as
Lukas mentioned,

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:26.650
there's a massive
amount of usage

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:29.600
when they kick up those
workloads as well.

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:31.870
We have teams
behind that are kind

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:34.420
of watching it and looking, and
making sure everything works

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:36.370
properly and as it should be.

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:39.800
And through our relationship
with Lukas-- myself

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:41.740
and engineering--
we are always kind

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:44.080
of making sure that these
things are ticking away

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:45.460
like they should be.

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:46.640
That's one example.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:48.389
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
now I'm curious about,

00:12:48.389 --> 00:12:51.700
how are you different or similar
to CREs, Customer Reliability

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:52.757
Engineer?

00:12:52.757 --> 00:12:53.590
MIKE ALTARACE: Yeah.

00:12:53.590 --> 00:12:55.570
That's a fair question,
a very good one.

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:58.390
I guess the main
difference is that we

00:12:58.390 --> 00:13:00.480
have an account-wide
relationship that

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:02.635
is continuous and constant.

00:13:02.635 --> 00:13:04.520
I'm always going to be there.

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:06.990
Even if there is going to
be one or more CREs engaged,

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:08.880
there's always going
to be a SCE that

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:13.020
oversees the technical aspect of
the relationship between Broad

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:14.890
Institute and Google.

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:17.580
So no matter what,
anything that has

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:20.261
to be with technical aspect of
those relationships, the SCE

00:13:20.261 --> 00:13:22.260
is going to be involved
and make sure everything

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:25.440
is moving in the right direction
and doesn't go orthogonal.

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:28.640
So either there are going to
be multiple engagements that

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:30.720
are in technical
nature through CREs,

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:33.930
through professional
services organizations.

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:38.250
But as a SCE, the
buck stops here

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:40.767
when it comes to technical
aspect of the relationship.

00:13:40.767 --> 00:13:43.350
And I'm always going to be there
to make sure it's working out

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:44.460
the way it should.

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:46.126
MARK MANDEL: So
actually for two things.

00:13:46.126 --> 00:13:48.890
Once, when you say
"ski," you mean an S-C.E.

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:50.045
That's just like--

00:13:50.045 --> 00:13:51.100
MIKE ALTARACE: Exactly.

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:53.610
Carl, my manager, is the one
responsible for coming up

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:54.510
with the SCE acronym.

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:59.550
So we actually have a SCE Lodge
in the Mountain View office

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:02.910
with skis and fake snow.

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:05.160
And we try to name
all of our services

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:09.420
and all our offerings in
kind of like the ski-related.

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:12.094
So our weekly report
is called the Snowboard

00:14:12.094 --> 00:14:13.510
MARK MANDEL: There's
a whole them.

00:14:13.510 --> 00:14:14.700
No, that's fantastic.

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:15.630
MIKE ALTARACE: There's
a ski camp for--

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:17.605
LUKAS KARLSSON: Ours is
called the Broadcast.

00:14:17.605 --> 00:14:18.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The Broadcast.

00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:19.177
That's very good.

00:14:19.177 --> 00:14:20.010
MARK MANDEL: Oh wow.

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:20.880
Nice.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:22.088
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I like puns.

00:14:22.088 --> 00:14:22.610
I like it.

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:24.030
MARK MANDEL: Pun-tastic.

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:27.489
So Mike, you were talking about
having a continuous engagement.

00:14:27.489 --> 00:14:28.530
What does that look like?

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:30.000
Do you have weekly meetings?

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:31.950
Are you in touch every day?

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:33.060
Does that work?

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:34.018
MIKE ALTARACE: Totally.

00:14:34.018 --> 00:14:37.482
We have we have scheduled weekly
cadences across multiple teams

00:14:37.482 --> 00:14:38.190
within the Broad.

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:40.800
Lukas, of course, is
a major part of it.

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:46.290
But it also means Hangouts,
and just ad hoc situations

00:14:46.290 --> 00:14:49.110
where we just get on the Hangout
and chat and discuss things.

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:52.110
So if something needs
to be addressed,

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:57.640
either from operation, product
guidance, connecting to a PM,

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:00.270
I get hit by the Broad,
or I hit the Broad,

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:04.230
and we just have an impromptu
conversation about it.

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:08.250
The personal and the
trust relationship--

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:09.930
the trust aspect of
the relationship--

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:13.860
is key fact to what we
consider being a success

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:16.020
and in kind of making
sure everything works

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:18.652
and reducing their anxiety
about using the platform.

00:15:18.652 --> 00:15:20.610
MARK MANDEL: So Lukas,
I was going to ask you--

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:22.050
and I think you're
about to jump in--

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:23.760
what does this look
like from your side?

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:25.770
LUKAS KARLSSON: Mike
has open office hours

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:30.510
that he provides via Hangout so
that anyone at the Broad who is

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:33.600
using GCP has access to Mike.

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:36.420
And in addition, we have
an internal Google group

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:39.930
of all of our GCP users
and an internal Slack

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:42.210
channel of the same users.

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:43.890
And Mike is involved
in both of those.

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:50.070
So many times, a user will
ask something in the chat room

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:51.720
or on the mailing list.

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:53.970
And the response is,
oh, Mike can help that.

00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:55.560
Just reach out to Mike.

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:57.660
Or Mike will even
see these things

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:01.620
because he's periodically active
on these channels with us.

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:04.710
So he's very much integrated
as a member of our team--

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:07.980
an extended member of the
Broad's Google Support Team.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:09.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:10.950
So that sounds awesome.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:13.020
And I guess that
many of our customers

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:16.640
that are out there wondering,
how this does happen?

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:18.750
How did it start?

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:21.480
LUKAS KARLSSON: The way we got
onto GCP in the first place

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:25.630
is because we were already
a G Suite customer.

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:29.130
And as we began
moving into the cloud

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:32.060
and exploring the multiple
offerings out there,

00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:33.720
you know, the G
Suite integration

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:36.850
with GCP, in that it's
using the same identities,

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:39.570
and we've already dealt with
the whole onboarding and

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:43.470
offboarding process of our users
so that, when someone leaves

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:46.080
the company and their G
Suite account is disabled,

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:49.980
that means they also lose access
to all of the GCP resources

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:51.180
that they would have had.

00:16:51.180 --> 00:16:52.770
So that's been
compelling for us.

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:55.050
And that sort of is
one of the contributing

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:57.820
factors to us using GCP.

00:16:57.820 --> 00:17:01.350
And certainly, over the
last four or five years

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:04.920
that we have been
a GCP customer,

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:08.130
our usage has grown
drastically over that time.

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:11.790
And sort of the
level of relationship

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:14.670
that we need to have in order
to be able to support that

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:16.650
has grown over that time.

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:21.599
So you know, I actually can't
imagine using the services

00:17:21.599 --> 00:17:23.220
at the scale that
we use them today

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:25.380
without having Mike
involved at this point.

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:27.480
And it's hard for
me to remember what

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:29.220
it was like before we had him.

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:31.159
I don't really like
to think about it.

00:17:31.159 --> 00:17:32.700
MARK MANDEL: So from
your side, Mike,

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:34.140
how did that look for you?

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:35.444
How did you get involved?

00:17:35.444 --> 00:17:37.110
What was the onboarding
process for you?

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:37.943
MIKE ALTARACE: Yeah.

00:17:37.943 --> 00:17:41.010
The tight relationship
between myself

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:44.760
as a SCE and the customer means
that the onboarding process

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:49.370
is almost like me doing a full
research about the customer,

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:53.250
their usage, reading up all the
internal documentation that we

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:57.000
had relating to the history
that we had with Broad

00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:00.110
before me joining,
and just going

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:04.270
and drilling as deep as
possible around their usage,

00:18:04.270 --> 00:18:07.160
their issues, their support
cases, the meeting notes.

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:09.380
Everything I could find
around the background

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:12.500
of Broad and Google
interaction, I

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:15.860
consumed it for a period of
time before I got engaged.

00:18:15.860 --> 00:18:19.390
And of course, from that point
on, I was introduced to Lukas.

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:22.070
And slowly, as
time went by, I got

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:25.210
introduced to other
members of Broad Institute.

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:28.760
And as issues came up, that
was another opportunity

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:32.090
to learn even more and dig
even deeper about things

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:32.930
that they do.

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:34.315
In particular,
for example, Broad

00:18:34.315 --> 00:18:37.370
is a big usage of
our Genomics API.

00:18:37.370 --> 00:18:41.330
So through the Broad, I actually
got a lot of opportunity

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:44.270
to learn and understand
how our Genomics API works.

00:18:44.270 --> 00:18:47.060
I got some data samples.

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:49.040
And I ran some of
the same workloads

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:52.790
that the Broad is running by
myself on our Genomics API

00:18:52.790 --> 00:18:55.520
so I can get a better
understanding of how

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:57.590
the things that they do
operate on our side, all

00:18:57.590 --> 00:19:00.680
the kind of pain points that
they're experiencing, so I can

00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:03.140
provide better assistance
and better understanding when

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:07.700
things happen to how
to quickly make it

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:08.950
solved as quickly as possible.

00:19:08.950 --> 00:19:10.533
LUKAS KARLSSON: And
then another thing

00:19:10.533 --> 00:19:12.380
that Mike does that's
really helpful is he

00:19:12.380 --> 00:19:15.530
sees all of the activity
on all of our tickets.

00:19:15.530 --> 00:19:17.820
So even if it's, like, in
the middle of the night,

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:20.660
and I go and submit a ticket
about some issue I just

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:23.930
discovered, when I chat
with Mike in the morning,

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:25.560
he's certainly aware of that.

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:27.530
And it's not like I
have to bring him up

00:19:27.530 --> 00:19:29.880
to speed on something
that just happened.

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:32.410
He has no full visibility
into everything.

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:35.440
So even times when
users within the Broad

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:38.300
submit tickets that
I don't know about,

00:19:38.300 --> 00:19:40.310
Mike has visibility into that.

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:42.710
So he can sometimes
make me aware of issues

00:19:42.710 --> 00:19:45.370
that I was not
aware of previously.

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:48.010
MIKE ALTARACE: And having that
additional context on the--

00:19:48.010 --> 00:19:50.540
I guess almost like the
metadata on the fact

00:19:50.540 --> 00:19:53.270
that Broad is a large customer,
and what they're doing,

00:19:53.270 --> 00:19:55.730
and what are the typical
issues they have--

00:19:55.730 --> 00:19:59.030
the ability to overlay
that, when issue comes up,

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:01.580
and communicate that to our
back end-- to engineering,

00:20:01.580 --> 00:20:03.980
to support, to PMs, whoever--

00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:07.460
makes the resolution and
the experience for the Broad

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:10.430
much better and much
more fluid than if it

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:13.470
has to be rediscovered
every time there's an issue.

00:20:13.470 --> 00:20:15.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I'm
curious about you mentioning

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:20.390
that you're able to file issues,
and that you might get them

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:21.710
basically directly.

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:24.920
Are those issues filed the
same way as any other customer?

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:26.970
Is there a special
relationship that you have?

00:20:26.970 --> 00:20:28.060
How does this work?

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:28.760
MIKE ALTARACE:
From the back end,

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:30.590
it's the same support
mechanism that all

00:20:30.590 --> 00:20:31.548
of our other customers.

00:20:31.548 --> 00:20:34.220
It's just the fact that I'm
tapped in right to the vein

00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:35.790
of the Broad activity.

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:39.110
So I see that in real
time as it happened.

00:20:39.110 --> 00:20:43.190
And if I find a place that
I can grease the skids,

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:45.590
so to speak, and make things
work a little bit more

00:20:45.590 --> 00:20:47.980
smoother by providing
additional context,

00:20:47.980 --> 00:20:49.077
I immediately get on that.

00:20:49.077 --> 00:20:51.410
MARK MANDEL: Mike, should we
talk at all about the issue

00:20:51.410 --> 00:20:56.047
tracker here, and how we have
additional visibility there.

00:20:56.047 --> 00:20:56.880
MIKE ALTARACE: Yeah.

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:58.310
That's actually a
very good point.

00:20:58.310 --> 00:21:01.580
So we actually, in addition
to just the general

00:21:01.580 --> 00:21:03.320
support ticketing
system, we actually

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:07.820
also provision
Broad as a partner

00:21:07.820 --> 00:21:10.550
to our internal
Buganizer system.

00:21:10.550 --> 00:21:15.350
So Broad has a unique component
within Google's internal bug

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:20.940
tracking system, which is a way
to get even deeper penetration

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:23.120
and understanding
of technical issues

00:21:23.120 --> 00:21:25.070
that are pertaining
to both sides.

00:21:25.070 --> 00:21:28.910
So Broad can talk
directly to, for example,

00:21:28.910 --> 00:21:32.510
Google Genomics API
engineers on specific issues

00:21:32.510 --> 00:21:35.600
through the additional
system of the Buganizer.

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:39.260
MARK MANDEL: So I'm putting on
my hey-I'm-a-GCP-customer hat.

00:21:39.260 --> 00:21:41.790
And I'm thinking to myself,
