00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:42.380
that you can share of,
like, how this actually

00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:46.830
works, like a concrete use case
where being a platinum customer

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:47.810
solved a big problem?

00:22:47.810 --> 00:22:51.680
Like I don't know if there
was some big technical problem

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:55.310
on your side, or I don't know--
like any kind of those issues

00:22:55.310 --> 00:22:58.042
where being a platinum customer
really made a difference?

00:22:58.042 --> 00:22:58.255
LUKAS KARLSSON: Yeah.

00:22:58.255 --> 00:22:59.796
I mean, the ones I
like to talk about

00:22:59.796 --> 00:23:02.900
are where we have
mistakenly exposed

00:23:02.900 --> 00:23:07.130
the lack of infinite
capacity that Google has.

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:08.600
So, like, there
was that first time

00:23:08.600 --> 00:23:10.100
where I got the
call where they were

00:23:10.100 --> 00:23:14.386
like, what are you doing in
the central region right now?

00:23:14.386 --> 00:23:15.510
And I was like, oh my gosh.

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:17.300
We just showed up
on Google's radar.

00:23:17.300 --> 00:23:18.950
That's amazing.

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:20.900
And we realized
that there actually

00:23:20.900 --> 00:23:22.550
are a limited
amount of resources,

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:25.234
and it's possible to consume
them all at some point.

00:23:25.234 --> 00:23:26.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:29.930
So now I'm wondering,
is there any story,

00:23:29.930 --> 00:23:32.510
any interesting
story you can share,

00:23:32.510 --> 00:23:35.300
that really shows
a big difference?

00:23:35.300 --> 00:23:39.320
Like, oh, luckily, we're a
platinum customer that we're

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:40.462
able to solve this issue.

00:23:40.462 --> 00:23:42.920
Is there something like that
that you can share that is not

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:44.830
top secret or something?

00:23:44.830 --> 00:23:45.920
LUKAS KARLSSON: Yeah.

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:49.490
So one of the things I think
that one of the reasons we are

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:52.540
a platinum customer is
the scale at which we run

00:23:52.540 --> 00:23:58.040
is such that we periodically
are able to expose

00:23:58.040 --> 00:24:03.850
the fact that the cloud
is not actually unlimited.

00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:07.760
So in one example, we
were using too many CPUs

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:09.500
in a particular
region, and we had

00:24:09.500 --> 00:24:11.750
to be very strategic
with Google about where

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:13.670
to move those workloads.

00:24:13.670 --> 00:24:16.040
There was another
situation last year

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:20.780
where we, in using all
of those preemptive VMs

00:24:20.780 --> 00:24:23.660
that we were using,
we managed to consume

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:27.750
most of the IP address resources
in a particular region.

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:31.400
And so we went down a very
strategic route with Google

00:24:31.400 --> 00:24:34.670
on not only tactically trying
to solve the immediate problem,

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:40.020
but basically trying to redesign
our application with Google

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:43.160
to make it so that we didn't
need to use so many external IP

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:44.060
addresses.

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:45.185
MARK MANDEL: And I assume--

00:24:45.185 --> 00:24:49.100
I assume it wasn't, like, 5
or 10 CPUs or IP addresses.

00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:50.720
It was a little
bit more than that?

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:53.540
LUKAS KARLSSON:
Tens of thousands.

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:55.970
So you know, one
of our applications

00:24:55.970 --> 00:24:58.050
needs to speak to the internet.

00:24:58.050 --> 00:25:01.670
And so while many of Google's
resources like storage

00:25:01.670 --> 00:25:05.290
are available using
internal IP addresses,

00:25:05.290 --> 00:25:07.970
if that same machine needs
to talk to, like, Docker hub,

00:25:07.970 --> 00:25:11.780
for example, then it
or something near it

00:25:11.780 --> 00:25:14.060
needs to have a public IP.

00:25:14.060 --> 00:25:17.420
So we sort of exposed
this limitation

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:20.390
in both Google's
infrastructure, as well as

00:25:20.390 --> 00:25:22.310
in the design of
our application.

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:25.070
And then Mike and his
team rallied around.

00:25:25.070 --> 00:25:27.890
This was actually during
Docker Con last year.

00:25:27.890 --> 00:25:30.980
And so there was a few
Googlers, like Mike's boss

00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:32.390
Karl was at Docker Con.

00:25:32.390 --> 00:25:35.355
And he and I were able to
get together in a little room

00:25:35.355 --> 00:25:36.980
and just sort of hack
through the issue

00:25:36.980 --> 00:25:39.800
with a bunch of Broadies who
were back on site in Cambridge,

00:25:39.800 --> 00:25:43.340
and with Mike and others
who were online at Google.

00:25:43.340 --> 00:25:45.290
And it was a really
collaborative, sort

00:25:45.290 --> 00:25:49.607
of urgent effort to try
and resolve this issue.

00:25:49.607 --> 00:25:51.440
MIKE ALTARACE: Just
from that point of view,

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:54.400
I mean, the back end
services and team owners

00:25:54.400 --> 00:25:58.060
are intimately aware of
Broad Institute because

00:25:58.060 --> 00:25:59.150
of issues like that.

00:25:59.150 --> 00:26:02.290
And it actually helped us
make the platform better.

00:26:02.290 --> 00:26:05.520
We changed a lot of
our internal processes,

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:09.640
and also general processes
as a cloud provider,

00:26:09.640 --> 00:26:12.005
as a result of these
type of interactions

00:26:12.005 --> 00:26:14.380
we had with the Broad Institute
being a platinum customer

00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:16.500
and really pushing
us to the limit.

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:18.190
And at the end of
the day, it really

00:26:18.190 --> 00:26:19.902
helps all of our customers.

00:26:19.902 --> 00:26:21.610
LUKAS KARLSSON: Another
example, I think,

00:26:21.610 --> 00:26:27.100
is Mike is able to help
me interact with many

00:26:27.100 --> 00:26:28.960
of the PMs of some
of the products

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:30.760
that we rely on very heavily.

00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:32.470
And through those
relationships, we're

00:26:32.470 --> 00:26:36.130
often able to get on
early access or alpha

00:26:36.130 --> 00:26:38.250
programs for certain products.

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:42.110
And for example, earlier this
year we were one of the first,

00:26:42.110 --> 00:26:45.550
if not the first, customer
to begin using a feature.

00:26:45.550 --> 00:26:48.430
And within the first day or
two of experimenting with it,

00:26:48.430 --> 00:26:50.680
we found two major bugs.

00:26:50.680 --> 00:26:53.620
And so Google was able to
sort of pull that feature,

00:26:53.620 --> 00:26:56.200
resolve those bugs,
and get that redeployed

00:26:56.200 --> 00:26:58.700
before any other
customers experienced it.

00:26:58.700 --> 00:27:01.930
So in the end, we were
able to get access

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:04.370
to a feature that
was critical to us,

00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:06.670
and we were able to help
Google make that feature more

00:27:06.670 --> 00:27:07.639
reliable for everybody.

00:27:07.639 --> 00:27:10.180
MIKE ALTARACE: And at the end,
taking that to the next level,

00:27:10.180 --> 00:27:12.166
as a result of all
