00:28:48.220 --> 00:28:49.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
I guess that it's

00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:50.960
time to go with a
question of the week.

00:28:50.960 --> 00:28:53.500
And as I was saying at the
beginning of the episode,

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:56.560
this one comes from
someone that came

00:28:56.560 --> 00:29:01.330
to see me at the Google Cloud
Platform booth at GopherCon

00:29:01.330 --> 00:29:03.040
last week in Denver.

00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:06.865
And his question was like,
OK, so I have a development

00:29:06.865 --> 00:29:08.800
cluster for Kubernetes.

00:29:08.800 --> 00:29:10.930
So basically, I'm running
Kubernetes on GKE.

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:14.920
And it's a development
QA thing, so I

00:29:14.920 --> 00:29:16.990
want it to be as
cheap as possible

00:29:16.990 --> 00:29:19.720
because there's no production
traffic going on in there.

00:29:19.720 --> 00:29:23.060
So I don't really care about
reliability or anything

00:29:23.060 --> 00:29:23.560
like that.

00:29:23.560 --> 00:29:26.660
So I can run it with
one single instance.

00:29:26.660 --> 00:29:29.260
And I can even run it with
[INAUDIBLE] more, which is

00:29:29.260 --> 00:29:30.970
actually very, very affordable.

00:29:30.970 --> 00:29:35.100
But then, when I want
to expose service,

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:38.270
if I expose it with the
default way of doing it,

00:29:38.270 --> 00:29:40.394
which is by saying type
is not load balancer,

00:29:40.394 --> 00:29:42.310
then all of a sudden,
you get a load balancer.

00:29:42.310 --> 00:29:44.560
And then you need to pay for
that load balancer, which

00:29:44.560 --> 00:29:48.130
is more than what they would
like to pay, specifically

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:49.760
for this test environment.

00:29:49.760 --> 00:29:55.340
So how can you expose service
without a load balancer?

00:29:55.340 --> 00:29:57.130
And I think that you
have a way to do it.

00:29:57.130 --> 00:30:00.304
So I guess you can go with one,
and I'll go with the other one.

00:30:00.304 --> 00:30:00.970
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:30:00.970 --> 00:30:04.210
I have one way that
I think should work.

00:30:04.210 --> 00:30:05.950
So the way that I
was going to say--

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:07.532
so we're saying
that there's one--

00:30:07.532 --> 00:30:09.990
we're going to have one instance
in our Kubernetes cluster.

00:30:09.990 --> 00:30:10.907
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:30:10.907 --> 00:30:12.364
MARK MANDEL: So
often, what happens

00:30:12.364 --> 00:30:14.350
if you're not running
Kubernetes in the cloud,

00:30:14.350 --> 00:30:17.410
rather than using a service
with a type load balancer,

00:30:17.410 --> 00:30:20.620
you often do what's called type
node port, which actually opens

00:30:20.620 --> 00:30:23.517
up the port directly
on the machine.

00:30:23.517 --> 00:30:25.600
And it opens up the same
port for all the services

00:30:25.600 --> 00:30:27.340
across all the machines that
are inside your cluster.

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:29.620
So then you could put your own
load balancer over the front.

00:30:29.620 --> 00:30:31.330
Just point it to those
ports across those machines

00:30:31.330 --> 00:30:32.030
and you're good to go.

00:30:32.030 --> 00:30:33.830
But there's nothing that
stops you from saying,

00:30:33.830 --> 00:30:35.020
hey, why don't we
just connect directly

00:30:35.020 --> 00:30:37.360
to that node port for
that individual service?

00:30:37.360 --> 00:30:40.180
So you could do a
service with a node port,

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:41.950
specify the port that
you want to open,

00:30:41.950 --> 00:30:42.980
and have that available.

00:30:42.980 --> 00:30:44.440
The only caveat I
would put is just

00:30:44.440 --> 00:30:47.140
to make sure that there is a
firewall rule on that Compute

00:30:47.140 --> 00:30:49.910
Engine instance that
GKE provisions for you

00:30:49.910 --> 00:30:53.000
to ensure that the traffic
will go through to that port.

00:30:53.000 --> 00:30:54.810
But in theory, it should work.

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:59.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I guess there's
a second caveat to that, which

00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:01.880
leads us to the other solution,
which is, what happens

00:31:01.880 --> 00:31:04.970
if that instance is restarted?

00:31:04.970 --> 00:31:06.290
You might get a different IP.

00:31:06.290 --> 00:31:06.600
MARK MANDEL: That's true.

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:08.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So all of a sudden,

00:31:08.710 --> 00:31:10.430
what IP should you target?

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:13.280
So that's why there's a
different solution, which

00:31:13.280 --> 00:31:15.860
is by using static IPs.

00:31:15.860 --> 00:31:19.340
So you can get a static
IP for your service.

00:31:19.340 --> 00:31:23.330
And then that static IP, it
is completely free of charge,

00:31:23.330 --> 00:31:25.307
as long as it's
assigned to something.

00:31:25.307 --> 00:31:27.765
So if you're running it with
an instance, then you're good.

00:31:27.765 --> 00:31:29.181
You don't need to
pay for anything

00:31:29.181 --> 00:31:30.650
for that one, which is cool.

00:31:30.650 --> 00:31:35.390
And then you can actually
set, on your ingress service,

00:31:35.390 --> 00:31:37.880
you can set that specific IP.

00:31:37.880 --> 00:31:42.680
The details to set this up are
actually part of a little repo

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:44.930
that Kelsey Hightower
open sourced.

00:31:44.930 --> 00:31:47.420
So we will have a link
to that specific place

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:51.950
that basically takes you through
how to reserve a static IP,

00:31:51.950 --> 00:31:54.650
and then how to add it to your
Kubernetes' configuration files

00:31:54.650 --> 00:31:57.230
in order to use that
one for your service

00:31:57.230 --> 00:31:59.022
rather than allocating
a new load balancer.

00:31:59.022 --> 00:32:00.938
MARK MANDEL: If you
wanted to get super fancy,

00:32:00.938 --> 00:32:03.290
you might be able to make
it preemptible as well.

00:32:03.290 --> 00:32:05.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
I tried, and you can.

00:32:05.650 --> 00:32:06.200
I don't know.

00:32:06.200 --> 00:32:08.295
I mean, it will be
more affordable.

00:32:08.295 --> 00:32:12.425
But at the same time, I wonder
if you're running tests on it--

00:32:12.425 --> 00:32:15.050
MARK MANDEL: But it's only going
to go down maybe once every 24

00:32:15.050 --> 00:32:15.689
hours or so.

00:32:15.689 --> 00:32:16.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Or more.

00:32:16.730 --> 00:32:17.780
MARK MANDEL: So it's not
going to happen that often.

00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:19.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's going
to make your tests flaky,

00:32:19.580 --> 00:32:21.155
so then you need to
rerun them again.

00:32:21.155 --> 00:32:22.030
MARK MANDEL: You
have to rerun them.

00:32:22.030 --> 00:32:24.655
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But at the same
time, it's integration tests..

00:32:24.655 --> 00:32:25.910
So probably, flakiness is OK.

00:32:25.910 --> 00:32:27.100
MARK MANDEL: I'm just
saying it's an option.

00:32:27.100 --> 00:32:27.510
It's there.

00:32:27.510 --> 00:32:29.343
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It is
definitely an option.

00:32:29.343 --> 00:32:31.490
The one that is not an
option that I thought about

00:32:31.490 --> 00:32:35.150
was, well, if I want to make
it as affordable as possible,

00:32:35.150 --> 00:32:37.790
maybe I can run an F1
micro because you get one

00:32:37.790 --> 00:32:40.230
for free per month.

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:41.430
So that would be perfect.

00:32:41.430 --> 00:32:45.140
But it is not enough to run
Kubernetes, unfortunately.

00:32:45.140 --> 00:32:47.540
So you need to run at
least F1 small, which

00:32:47.540 --> 00:32:50.110
is also very, very, very
affordable, to be honest.

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:53.196
MARK MANDEL: So you can run
Kubernetes, but no paths.

00:32:53.196 --> 00:32:54.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Basically, yeah.

00:32:54.570 --> 00:32:56.620
You can run Kubernetes, but
nothing will run inside.

00:32:56.620 --> 00:32:57.120
So yeah.

00:32:57.120 --> 00:32:58.562
Not big enough.

00:32:58.562 --> 00:32:59.520
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:00.020
All right.

